# Caddy + the Cloudflare DNS-01 provider — the estate's only in-repo image build.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS (H1, ADR 017 amended). Once E6 closes 80/443, Caddy can answer
# neither an http-01 nor a tls-alpn-01 ACME challenge, so Let's Encrypt renewal
# for llunde.no / www / api breaks. The current certs were issued 2026-08-09 and
# expire 2026-11-07; Caddy starts renewing ~30 days out, i.e. ~2026-10-08. That
# is the real deadline — not the cutover date. DNS-01 is the only challenge type
# that needs no inbound port, and it needs a provider module compiled IN: the
# official image ships none and Caddy has no runtime plugin loading.
#
# Warm certs are what keep break-glass cheap: re-open the ports, flip DNS back to
# the A records, and the origin already holds valid certificates for all three
# names instead of racing Let's Encrypt during an outage.
#
# It also closes a gap this work uncovered: the front door was running the
# FLOATING `docker.io/library/caddy:2` tag while cloudflared beside it was
# digest-pinned. It was stable only by accident — quadlet's `Pull=missing` meant
# the local image cache hid the drift, so a reprovision or a `podman image prune`
# could have swapped the front-door binary with no diff anywhere in this repo.
#
# TWO STAGES, ONE VERSION. Argument-less `xcaddy build` compiles the version in
# the builder image's CADDY_VERSION env, and the runtime image supplies the
# entrypoint, /etc/caddy layout and /data storage. Pinning only one side would
# quietly produce a caddy that does not match the image built around it — so both
# FROMs take the same ARG, and CI asserts `caddy version` after the build.
ARG CADDY_VERSION=2.11.4

FROM docker.io/library/caddy:${CADDY_VERSION}-builder AS builder

# Version-pinned like every other dependency in this estate; bumps are deliberate
# edits, never a floating fetch at build time.
RUN xcaddy build --with github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare@v0.2.4

FROM docker.io/library/caddy:${CADDY_VERSION}
COPY --from=builder /usr/bin/caddy /usr/bin/caddy
